Civgraph — licensing scope
==========================

The MIT licence in LICENSE covers the SOFTWARE in this repository: the
application source, build scripts, Cloudflare Pages Functions, tests and
documentation written for this project.

It does NOT relicense the DATA.

Why the distinction matters here
--------------------------------

This repository tracks a large amount of third-party material — census tables,
scanned books, boundary metadata and map catalogues — obtained from bodies
including NISRA, the CSO, OSNI/Land & Property Services, Ordnance Survey
Ireland/Tailte Éireann, PRONI, the Northern Ireland Assembly and the Houses of
the Oireachtas, as well as from individual contributors.

That material arrives under its own terms — predominantly the Open Government
Licence and Creative Commons Attribution, which are attribution licences with
conditions of their own. Those terms travel with the data. Nobody redistributing
this repository, including the copyright holder, can substitute the MIT licence
for them, and the MIT grant above should not be read as attempting to.

Where the per-source terms are recorded
---------------------------------------

Licence and attribution for each dataset are carried in the catalogue metadata
rather than in this file, because that is where they stay accurate:

    data/database/maps.json       per-layer licence, provider and source
    data/database/sources.json    source records and download provenance

Those files currently record several hundred Creative Commons Attribution
references and several dozen Open Government Licence references. Treat the
entry for a given layer as authoritative for that layer.

Bundled dependencies
--------------------

The browser bundle in app/build includes third-party open-source packages under
MIT, BSD-3-Clause and Apache-2.0 licences. Their required copyright and
permission notices are reproduced in:

    app/build/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt

That file is generated from the esbuild metafile by
scripts/build/notices.mjs and is verified by `npm run check`.

If you are reusing this work
----------------------------

Reusing the code: MIT applies. Keep the copyright and permission notice.

Reusing the data: check the per-source terms in the catalogue first. Most of it
requires attribution to the originating body, and some sources place further
conditions on redistribution. Attribution to Civgraph alone is not sufficient
where the underlying source requires its own credit.
